module gunbc.tailscale_join_converge

// EVERY ENROLLED HOST IS A MEMBER OF THE TAILNET, OR THE CONVERGE REFUSES -- AND THE ROUTER KNOWS IT BY NAME.
//
// WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS. The router addresses its upstreams by TAILNET NAME
// (gunbc.fleet.fleet_tailnet::tailnet_host_from, the single authority for what a host is called on the
// tailnet), which is what makes an IP change need no code change. That address is only reachable if the host
// is a MEMBER, and as of 2026-11-15 srv3, srv4, h11-a16 and butch were not members as srv1 sees them, with
// h11 not even having the binary installed. Nothing in the fleet converged that: the machine-name converge
// (gunbc.tailscale_machine_name_converge) can only rename a device that has already joined, and `tailscale
// set` on an unjoined node has no prefs to change. So membership was a hand step on a keyboard -- the kind of
// step performed once, forgotten on the next host, and discovered when the router cannot resolve anything.
//
// WHAT IT CONVERGES, PER HOST, IN ORDER: the package (installed if absent, through the admitted apt route),
// the credential file (placed by the one custody authority, never by this module writing a file itself), the
// membership (`tailscale up`, with the auth key read from its FILE and never from argv), and then the
// read-back -- not "the command exited 0", which upstream's own `up` can answer with a login that never
// completed, but the two facts the router actually depends on, asked FROM THE ROUTER: the host's tailnet name
// RESOLVES on srv1 through NSS, and it ANSWERS a tailnet-only ping.
//
// IDEMPOTENCE IS THE DESIGN, NOT A MODE. A host already Running is a Noop with no mutation and no credential
// placement. The ordering matters more than it looks: the credential is a REUSABLE enrollment key, so a
// converge that touched it on every run of every host would spread a fleet-wide secret across machines that
// joined long ago, on the theory that reading a status line is cheap. The status read comes first, and the
// key file is placed only on the arm that will use it.
//
// ONE AUTHORITY FOR THE NAME, END TO END. The hostname the node is told to claim and the name the router is
// asked about are both projections of tailnet_host_from(HostIdentity) -- the first through
// canonical_hostname_for_fleet_slot, the second through the labels that function joins. There is no string in
// this module that is a tailnet name because someone typed it, which is the failure that makes a join
// "succeed" on a node nobody can find.
//
// WHAT IT REFUSES, AND WHY EACH REFUSAL IS NOT A FAILURE TO HANDLE:
//   A HOST WITH NO ALLOCATED HOSTNAME. tailnet_host_from is Absent, so there is no name to join under and
//     none to read back; joining under the OS hostname would create a member the router will never address,
//     and it would do it quietly.
//   AN EXPIRED AUTH KEY, consulted on the arm that would use it (gunbc.tailscale_fleet_auth_key::
//     tailscale_fleet_auth_key_usable_on). An expired key fails the login, and a login failure reads like a
//     network fault; refusing on the calendar says which one it was. A host that is already a member is a
//     Noop regardless, because upstream is explicit that expiry stops enrollments and not membership.
//   A BACKEND STATE THAT IS NOT RUNNING, including Starting and NeedsMachineAuth. The fleet's key is
//     pre-approved, so NeedsMachineAuth means the key is not what the model says it is -- a custody and
//     roster question, not something to retry past.
//   AN UNREADABLE PACKAGE QUERY OR STATUS. dpkg-query's "unknown" and a status document that will not parse
//     are refusals, never defaults: "not installed" and "installed" produce opposite legs, and a guessed one
//     installs or skips a package on a host nobody looked at.
//   A TAG MISMATCH BETWEEN THE KEY AND THE JOIN. Upstream says a tagged key's node "assumes the identity of
//     the auth key's tags", so advertising a different tag would not add a role -- it would leave the node in
//     the key's tag while the plan claimed another, and every reachability judgment this converge then makes
//     would be about a tag the node is not in.
//   A PING THAT ANSWERS AS SOMEBODY ELSE. The read-back compares the node name in the pong against the
//     expected tailnet name and refuses on a mismatch: an address answering from a stale peer record is not
//     the host that was just joined.
//   ANYTHING THE TYPED SURFACES REFUSE. The argv builders, the custody row and placement, the ssh targets,
//     the sealed write and the portable-word check all carry their own refusal arms, and this module
//     propagates them. No leg here is ever composed as a shell string.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: approve a device in the admin console (the key is pre-approved, which is the operator
// decision that makes the join non-interactive); restart or reauthenticate a daemon (`--force-reauth` is not
// offered by extdeps.tailscale.join at all, because it may drop the connection it is being run over); or
// render the router's upstream configuration, which is held for its own read-only diff.

